** Local Endpoint Protection **

Is the hardening of endpoint firmware and boot processes.
Most security practices focus on securing devices.

Threats have been emerging that attack the firmware and boot processes of endpoint devices.

Physically securing devices so that attackers do not have physical access is extremely important. It is much easier to compromise a traditional computer system
if you have physical access because many devices have an administrator account reset procedure that requires only physical access to the device.

Locking down the basic input/output system (BIOS) and other boot-time systems can prevent these types pf attacks from being successful.

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

Firmware is the software that usually run from a chip on the endpoint.
This software is responsible for detecting and reporting hardware connected to the device.
After the firmware performs all the hardware checks, it assists in loading the operating system. Modern computers usually use either
the legacy basic input output system (BIOS) or the newer unified extensible firmware interface (UEFI) Both perform similar functions,
but UEFI is much newer and usually incorporates a graphical interface and more robust security features

